<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Ziegenhain</h1><p class="st-meta">1144 CE – 1450 CE (approx.) · capital Ziegenhain</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Hessian county in the Schwalm valley whose counts held a moated castle and whose extinction in 1450 enlarged the landgraviate of Hesse.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of Ziegenhain appear around 1144, with a moated castle in the marshy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schwalm">Schwalm</a> valley. Through marriage they also acquired the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidda">Nidda</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetterau">Wetterau</a> in the 12th century. Their lands lay between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/landgraviate-of-hesse">Landgraviate of Hesse</a> and the abbeys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> and Hersfeld.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts were advocates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda-abbey">Fulda Abbey</a> and often in conflict with the landgraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. Their territory was rural, centred on the small town of Ziegenhain and the castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidda">Nidda</a>. Little is known of their administration in detail.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last count, John II, died in 1450, and both Ziegenhain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidda">Nidda</a> passed to the landgraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. Landgrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-landgrave-of-hesse">Philip the Magnanimous</a> later turned Ziegenhain into a fortress, used as a prison in later centuries.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-ziegenhain.jpg" alt="County of Ziegenhain" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Dirk Schmidt (Celsius auf Wikivoyage) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schwalmstadt_treysa_rathaus_ds_wv_09_2010.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1144 CE – 1450 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ziegenhain</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John II of Ziegenhain</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hessian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ziegenhain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1297&amp;focus=county-of-ziegenhain" aria-label="County of Ziegenhain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4889 412 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Ziegenhain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5209 668L5229 694M5209 668L5215 656"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5215 656h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5209 668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5209 668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1297&amp;focus=county-of-ziegenhain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
